00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:17.220
And I did a lot more reading

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:19.580
and the pieces kind of fell together for me,

00:13:19.580 --> 00:13:22.780
but it was a long gradual place for me to get to

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:26.820
where now I think it's my stance is that

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:31.850
we need to build teams of people that are safe

00:13:32.650 --> 00:13:34.530
and engaged and there's ways to do that.

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:36.290
And that's where great results come from,

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:37.810
whether it's software or, you know,

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:39.770
it doesn't matter what the product is

00:13:39.770 --> 00:13:44.770
rather than micromanaging and beating people up verbally

00:13:46.250 --> 00:13:47.950
or however.

00:13:47.950 --> 00:13:50.470
So it took me, it was a while for me to get there.

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:52.750
I stopped being an asshole almost right away

00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:53.590
to be clear,

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:57.470
but it took me a while to really figure out

00:13:57.470 --> 00:13:59.390
this empathy part,

00:13:59.390 --> 00:14:01.710
but that also was the first book recommended to me.

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:03.750
I kind of, okay, I get what they're saying,

00:14:03.750 --> 00:14:05.850
but I didn't know how to apply it.

00:14:05.850 --> 00:14:08.290
And it wasn't until I did a lot more reading

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:10.450
and thinking and even observing leaders

00:14:10.450 --> 00:14:12.490
who I thought were doing a good job

00:14:12.490 --> 00:14:13.730
and also thinking about leaders

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:14.810
who did not do a good job,

00:14:14.810 --> 00:14:17.500
but I didn't like about them, but I got there.

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:18.900
So that's my story there.

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:20.700
It's a gradual thing.

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:22.500
One thing you hit on the very end of your answer

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:24.340
that I wanna talk a little bit more about

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:25.780
is in the newsletter,

00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:27.180
you talked about the hesitant yes,

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:28.500
that's uncomfortable for a quarter.

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:31.700
Like, yeah, people kind of live with that and whatever.

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:35.540
And then versus the no, that's uncomfortable right now

00:14:35.540 --> 00:14:36.380
and then goes away.

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:38.900
Can you talk a little bit more about that concept

00:14:38.900 --> 00:14:42.100
and yeah, I'll just leave it at that.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:44.340
Yeah, yeah, totally.

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:45.740
First of all, I just wanna comment.

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:47.580
I love that that was your first book too

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:48.900
and the one handed by a mentor.

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:51.580
We have that in common.

00:14:51.580 --> 00:14:53.940
And like you, I didn't really quite know

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:55.900
how to apply some of the stuff in there.

00:14:55.900 --> 00:14:57.260
There were a few items, right?

00:14:57.260 --> 00:14:58.700
I could apply almost immediately,

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:01.420
but a lot of it seemed a bit fluffy.

00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:03.140
Not anything against the book.

00:15:03.140 --> 00:15:05.380
It just for the way my brain thinks.

00:15:05.380 --> 00:15:07.460
I think in systems, I think in code,

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:10.340
I needed something speaking more to that.

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:15.620
To your question though about the no versus the yes

00:15:15.620 --> 00:15:17.100
and having that discomfort,

00:15:17.980 --> 00:15:19.980
I've been in a number of deployments

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:22.460
and working to implement different systems.

00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:25.580
The technology of course has changed over the years.

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:27.220
This particular scenario though

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:29.620
and it is actually based off of a real story.

00:15:29.620 --> 00:15:31.020
I wasn't making this up.

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:34.100
This is a real situation I was in.

00:15:34.100 --> 00:15:37.090
And having that hesitant yes

00:15:37.090 --> 00:15:39.850
or that assumption of a yes and having that briefly

00:15:39.850 --> 00:15:42.970
and then it just kind of drags out and it festers.

00:15:42.970 --> 00:15:44.490
People are being polite

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:46.330
and you're not giving that out for them

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:47.410
to be able to speak up

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:51.010
or to maybe have that conversation separately

00:15:51.010 --> 00:15:53.930
where there's a smaller room and they feel more open,

00:15:53.930 --> 00:15:56.930
psychologically safe to open up.

00:15:56.930 --> 00:15:59.730
If you don't get that space to allow for that,

00:16:01.180 --> 00:16:06.020
it's going to turn into tests that perhaps get obstructed.

00:16:06.020 --> 00:16:08.060
People don't have time to do it.

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:09.660
UAT doesn't happen.

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:12.300
There's constant scope creep in the system.

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:15.100
Things just do not move as smoothly.

00:16:15.100 --> 00:16:17.380
If somebody can get their objection upfront,

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:19.060
the no and it's uncomfortable

00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:21.980
and you can get to the root of it and talk about it,

00:16:21.980 --> 00:16:24.260
sure, maybe you slow down for a day or two,

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:27.420
but you get that out of the way and you move through it

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:30.740
and you hear them, you empathize with them.

00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:34.540
You can adjust early on, be agile with your project,

00:16:34.540 --> 00:16:37.860
make the changes necessary, get more information,

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:39.220
calm their fears.

00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:40.780
I have found nine times out of 10,

00:16:40.780 --> 00:16:43.700
these fears or concerns or no that get brought up,

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:46.500
the objections, this is because of a lack of understanding

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:49.420
about downstream, what is actually happening

00:16:49.420 --> 00:16:51.700
and what the tool or what the implementation means

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:53.220
for them or their team.

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:56.380
It's not usually a no that you can't get past.

00:16:56.380 --> 00:16:58.920
It's better to deal with it head on

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:01.230
and then progress through.

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:03.270
Yeah, there's a lot of goodness in there.

00:17:03.270 --> 00:17:05.910
It's something I, again, every time you start talking,

00:17:05.910 --> 00:17:08.550
my brain goes, oh, and this and this and this.

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:12.870
And Amazon has this thing and often it's abused

00:17:12.870 --> 00:17:16.030
as these one sentence principles usually are,

00:17:16.190 --> 00:17:18.990
disagree and commit, which is good in principle,

00:17:18.990 --> 00:17:20.830
but bad in abuse.

00:17:20.830 --> 00:17:23.190
But what's good about it is,

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:25.190
and this requires psychological safety,

00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:28.870
you need to be able to, anyone on the team should be able

00:17:28.870 --> 00:17:34.130
to disagree with decisions and more importantly, voice why.

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:38.300
It is so much easier to take that quiet no

00:17:38.300 --> 00:17:40.180
if you understand, well, it's not even,

00:17:40.180 --> 00:17:41.420
it's a loud obnoxious no

00:17:41.420 --> 00:17:44.450
if you don't explain the why behind it.

00:17:44.450 --> 00:17:49.450
And when you can say no, and for reasons,

00:17:50.170 --> 00:17:52.050
and we can agree on the reasons that make sense

00:17:52.050 --> 00:17:57.340
and disagree on maybe on some of the nuance,

00:17:57.900 --> 00:17:59.900
people wanna have their voices heard.

00:17:59.900 --> 00:18:01.620
And I think that's where some of that,

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:04.510
I mean, that's some of the empathy I've learned

00:18:04.510 --> 00:18:08.670
is just people want to be heard, not told what to do.

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:11.230
And I, and again, the buzz,

00:18:11.230 --> 00:18:12.990
and I reflect on this a lot,

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:15.820
something I've said for a long time is,

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:18.060
in times of stress, people will revert

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:20.220
to the true versions of themselves.

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:23.940
And I had done a lot of work on myself

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:26.660
to become who I am, not just,

00:18:26.660 --> 00:18:27.620
I'm not even thinking career wise,

00:18:27.620 --> 00:18:29.980
who I am as a person.

00:18:29.980 --> 00:18:32.420
And when I get overwhelmed,

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:36.120
I will fall into the same traps.

00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:38.040
I won't turn into an asshole anymore.

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:40.040
I beat that part out, but I can be,

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:43.420
I'll be very transparent here.

00:18:44.260 --> 00:18:46.300
If there's stress going on, I have a lot on my mind,

00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:50.660
I can fall into insecurity, lack of confidence,

00:18:52.380 --> 00:18:54.060
little self-centered almost.

00:18:55.700 --> 00:18:57.900
And it's important as leaders,

00:18:57.900 --> 00:18:59.380
we recognize that that can happen.

00:18:59.380 --> 00:19:00.340
This is the buzz.

00:19:00.340 --> 00:19:01.700
Like now I notice that as buzz and I go,

00:19:01.700 --> 00:19:04.190
okay, let me take a step back

00:19:04.190 --> 00:19:05.910
and think about where I am and why,

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:07.830
and see if I can reset.

00:19:07.830 --> 00:19:11.390
And as a leader, that has to happen all the time

00:19:11.390 --> 00:19:13.750
because you're leading people, not,

00:19:13.750 --> 00:19:15.430
I mean, I wanna say people, not projects,

00:19:15.430 --> 00:19:16.630
but I'm going to say that, yeah.

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:19.030
We're leading people, people get the projects done.

00:19:19.030 --> 00:19:22.660
But if you're a leader, it's about creating

00:19:22.660 --> 00:19:24.900
that environment where people feel safe

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:26.340
to do their best work.

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:29.020
So that is a very long transition

00:19:29.020 --> 00:19:30.820
into a question around empathy.

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:33.380
And you and I both had our journeys

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:34.540
on how to learn empathy.

00:19:34.540 --> 00:19:37.220
And we probably didn't think,

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:39.580
if I can go back to whatever age person

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:41.420
that was in me at the time and think,

00:19:41.420 --> 00:19:43.220
I wouldn't think I have no empathy.

00:19:44.140 --> 00:19:46.740
But I certainly have a lot more now than I did then.

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:52.340
I believe that empathy is learnable.

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:53.460
We both done that.

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:57.940
But have you come across folks who it's more difficult?

00:19:57.940 --> 00:19:59.780
Like how, I'll put it this way.

00:19:59.780 --> 00:20:01.580
What is your advice to leaders,

00:20:01.580 --> 00:20:04.220
regardless of how empathic they think they are

00:20:04.220 --> 00:20:06.870
to become more empathetic?

00:20:06.870 --> 00:20:09.030
Yeah, that's an interesting question, Alan,

00:20:09.030 --> 00:20:13.740
because often the perception is

00:20:13.740 --> 00:20:15.780
we do think we're empathetic

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:17.980
or of course I'm a good person.

00:20:17.980 --> 00:20:18.820
I'm a good leader.

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:19.660
I'm good at this.

00:20:19.660 --> 00:20:22.500
We have that bias about ourselves.

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:24.300
The exercise that you explained earlier,

00:20:24.300 --> 00:20:25.820
having a 360.

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:27.700
I mean, that's a good starting point.

00:20:27.700 --> 00:20:29.500
Having open dialogue,

00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:32.620
having psychological safety

00:20:32.620 --> 00:20:35.380
where you allow people who are reporting to you

00:20:35.380 --> 00:20:38.980
or around you to speak open and candidly with you

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:40.860
so you can improve.

00:20:40.860 --> 00:20:42.260
I know for myself,

00:20:42.260 --> 00:20:45.420
I consider myself to have high level empathy.

00:20:45.420 --> 00:20:46.900
I have had to work on it

00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:49.580
and how I express that with emotional intelligence

00:20:49.580 --> 00:20:51.980
and developing the skill,

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:53.820
just like my technical skills,

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:55.460
it's something to be developed.

00:20:56.780 --> 00:20:57.620
But even me,

00:20:57.620 --> 00:20:59.860
I've had to have open and honest feedback.

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:00.820
Like I'll be truthful.

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:03.860
One area of feedback that I've had is sometimes,

00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:06.640
Collette, you can be too direct.

00:21:06.640 --> 00:21:09.880
For me, I like direct communication

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:11.000
when somebody talks to me.

00:21:11.920 --> 00:21:13.080
We also have that in common.

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:13.920
Okay, here we go.

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:14.800
Yeah, I'm like,

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:17.400
if you can say it in two sentences instead of five,

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:19.040
please do so because that's quicker,

00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:19.880
it's more efficient

00:21:19.880 --> 00:21:21.040
and then you're getting to the point

00:21:21.040 --> 00:21:23.040
and just tell me exactly what you need.

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:24.400
That is what I prefer.

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:26.280
And sometimes I fall into that,

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:27.800
especially when I am stressed,

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:29.360
I'm in a hurry.

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:31.680
I got several meetings back to back

00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:33.820
and I'm trying to get something done.

00:21:33.820 --> 00:21:35.660
But I have discovered it is sometimes better

00:21:35.660 --> 00:21:37.820
to delay perhaps a response.

00:21:37.820 --> 00:21:38.860
Like for me personally,

00:21:38.860 --> 00:21:41.460
I've had to delay a response like an email

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:44.740
or maybe pushing out a call if I'm in that state

00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:46.540
because if I know my audience

00:21:46.540 --> 00:21:48.100
and the folks I'm gonna be talking to

00:21:48.100 --> 00:21:50.940
who do not prefer that type of communication,

00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:53.420
I need to be in the right mindset.

00:21:53.420 --> 00:21:56.060
So what do I advise for leaders

00:21:56.060 --> 00:21:57.300
or just people in general

00:21:57.300 --> 00:21:59.340
who are really trying to develop this, right?

00:21:59.340 --> 00:22:01.500
Number one, I mean, getting feedback,

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:03.260
just recapping that.

00:22:03.260 --> 00:22:07.500
Number two, know that we all have that inherent bias.

00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:11.260
And number three, know that this is a skill to develop

00:22:11.260 --> 00:22:12.660
just like any other skill.

00:22:12.660 --> 00:22:15.380
I mean, your technical skills, ability to code,

00:22:15.380 --> 00:22:17.980
whatever you do, leading people.

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:19.940
These are things that did not come overnight,

00:22:19.940 --> 00:22:21.100
you had to develop them.

00:22:21.100 --> 00:22:23.100
This is a skill to proactively develop

00:22:23.100 --> 00:22:24.780
just like anything else,

00:22:24.780 --> 00:22:26.540
the human skills element.

00:22:26.540 --> 00:22:27.780
And it is important.

00:22:28.660 --> 00:22:29.900
Alan, just like you said, right?

00:22:29.900 --> 00:22:31.460
Like with teams, yeah, okay,

00:22:31.460 --> 00:22:33.300
projects and you have projects,

00:22:33.300 --> 00:22:35.140
but really it's about the people

00:22:35.140 --> 00:22:37.380
who are working together to make it happen,

00:22:38.260 --> 00:22:39.580
who are putting in their time,

00:22:39.580 --> 00:22:41.260
putting in their knowledge,

00:22:41.260 --> 00:22:43.860
working together cohesively.

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:46.260
You could have all the best tech in the world

00:22:46.260 --> 00:22:48.420
and people with the highest skills in the world,

00:22:48.420 --> 00:22:50.380
but if they're not meshing together,

00:22:50.380 --> 00:22:52.340
if you're not leading them in a way

00:22:52.340 --> 00:22:55.900
that is conducive to them doing their best work,

00:22:55.900 --> 00:22:57.500
it's not going to matter

00:22:57.500 --> 00:22:59.300
how many hard skills you have.

00:22:59.300 --> 00:23:01.180
A million percent, yes.

00:23:01.180 --> 00:23:02.020
Yeah.

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:07.020
I, and I don't think enough leaders realize that.

00:23:07.060 --> 00:23:09.420
I had a leader once, he was,

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:11.100
it's a long story, acquaintance of mine.

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:14.780
He ran a team and the team was not performing.

00:23:14.780 --> 00:23:16.380
This team's all horrible.

00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:18.260
And in the Microsoft way,

00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:19.740
what they did was they transferred this leader

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:21.740
to another organization.

00:23:21.740 --> 00:23:24.940
And he got there and he said the same thing.

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:26.220
Oh, these guys are supposed to be good.

00:23:26.220 --> 00:23:27.060
They're not any good.

00:23:27.060 --> 00:23:28.380
They can't do anything.

00:23:28.380 --> 00:23:31.580
And then he was finally let go

00:23:31.580 --> 00:23:34.340
because the problem was not with him.

00:23:34.340 --> 00:23:36.060
You know, that lack of self-awareness.

00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:41.830
You need like, I believe like, you know, little story.

00:23:42.270 --> 00:23:44.310
There are smart people everywhere.

00:23:44.310 --> 00:23:46.750
It's not just that they're not isolated to any one,

00:23:46.750 --> 00:23:48.230
they're not isolated to the fan companies.

00:23:48.230 --> 00:23:50.510
There are people that would,

00:23:50.510 --> 00:23:52.510
and I can say it's working at Microsoft for so long.

00:23:52.510 --> 00:23:55.190
There are people not at Microsoft, for example,

00:23:55.190 --> 00:23:59.230
who mop the floor with tech skills

00:23:59.230 --> 00:24:00.830
over some of the Microsoft folks I work with.

00:24:00.830 --> 00:24:02.070
It's not that they're all concentrated.

00:24:02.070 --> 00:24:05.990
But what I've seen teams really deliver

00:24:06.030 --> 00:24:07.150
in my time since then,

00:24:07.150 --> 00:24:11.310
is because they've cared about what they're doing.

00:24:11.310 --> 00:24:14.350
And if you read like the Gallup research on engagement,

00:24:14.350 --> 00:24:15.670
employees only need three things

00:24:15.670 --> 00:24:17.230
to be engaged in their work.

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:19.110
And engagement means they're gonna do really good work.

00:24:19.110 --> 00:24:22.310
They want feedback.

00:24:22.310 --> 00:24:23.150
We talked about that.

00:24:23.150 --> 00:24:24.950
And building a culture of feedback is important.

00:24:24.950 --> 00:24:26.430
They wanna know why their job's important.

00:24:26.430 --> 00:24:27.390
They don't wanna be the person

00:24:27.390 --> 00:24:29.190
putting caps on pens in a widget factory.

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:33.550
They wanna know like, what is, why is what I do important?

00:24:33.550 --> 00:24:34.950
And they wanna know their manager cares

00:24:34.950 --> 00:24:36.190
about them and their career.

00:24:36.190 --> 00:24:37.030
That's it.

00:24:37.030 --> 00:24:41.790
It's so easy, but so many leaders ignore those things.

00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:45.230
When I see those things happen, teams just crush it.

00:24:45.230 --> 00:24:46.070
And this is true.

00:24:46.070 --> 00:24:48.750
This is not just true for software tech stuff.

00:24:48.750 --> 00:24:50.270
One thing I wanna dive into a little bit more,

00:24:50.270 --> 00:24:52.350
that was a little bonus story there,

00:24:52.350 --> 00:24:54.950
is these soft skills or human skills.

00:24:54.950 --> 00:24:56.030
I like human skills better.

